WEBVTT

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.800
This is the story of NVIDIA, and one investment idea.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:09.450
In 1993, Jensen Huang co-founded NVIDIA.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.710
At first, the company made graphics chips for video games.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.380
Today, artificial intelligence, or AI, is part of everyday life.

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:26.970
People use it to answer questions, write letters, translate, and plan trips.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.740
Even an 80-year-old retiree can ask AI for help, just by typing or talking.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.660
But every AI answer needs a huge amount of computing power.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.020
A regular computer chip works on a few tasks at a time.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:51.830
A GPU, the kind of chip NVIDIA makes, works on thousands of small tasks at once.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.460
That is exactly the kind of work AI needs.

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:00.560
So why is NVIDIA so important?

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:04.950
Think of the gold rush. Many people went looking for gold.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:09.980
But the people selling the shovels did well, no matter who found the gold.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:17.770
In today's AI rush, NVIDIA sells the shovels: the chips that many AI systems are built on.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.370
The biggest technology companies buy them to build their AI.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.800
Those chips go into giant buildings called data centers.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:33.580
Each one is filled with thousands of computers, running day and night.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.420
This is where AI really lives.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:41.850
And this demand shows up in NVIDIA's sales.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.880
Three recent quarters tell the story.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:48.510
Sales of $68.1 billion,

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:51.370
then $81.6 billion,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.230
then $96.2 billion.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:59.210
From the first to the third, that is growth of about 41%.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:08.560
NVIDIA works with major partners: cloud companies, AI labs, carmakers, and governments.

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:13.180
And soon, NVIDIA will report its next quarterly results.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.640
Results like these can move the share price, sometimes a lot.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.330
Now, the investment idea.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.630
Imagine buying NVIDIA shares at $218 each.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:35.490
If the price rises 8%, each share becomes worth $235.44.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:40.710
That is a gain of $17.44 on every share.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.250
This idea uses borrowed money, which is called leverage.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.840
You put in $25,000 of your own.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.410
Your broker lends you about $100,000 more.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:02.560
Together, that buys 572 shares, worth about $125,000.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.660
So your money controls about five times more shares than it could alone.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:11.740
Now, the result.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.560
By investing $25,000 this way,

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.330
if the price rises 8%,

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:25.370
you could potentially make $9,975.68.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.980
That is about 40% on your own money.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.230
And with $50,000 of your own, plus more borrowing,

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.540
you could buy 1,720 shares.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:48.090
The same 8% rise could potentially make $29,996.80.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.180
That one uses about seven and a half times leverage.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:04:00.530
One thing to remember: borrowed money makes gains bigger, and it makes losses bigger too.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:06.150
So that is NVIDIA: the company selling the shovels of the AI age.
